-- Q3612: Security and Control Frameworks [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Identifying where a single control satisfies requirements across multiple frameworks","Eliminating all overlapping regulatory requirements from the compliance program","Replacing multiple frameworks with a single proprietary compliance framework","Ignoring duplicate compliance obligations to reduce the total audit scope"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per the NIST Cybersecurity Framework Core and COBIT 2019 mapping guidance, control mapping identifies overlapping requirements across multiple frameworks so that a single control implementation can satisfy several compliance obligations simultaneously. This reduces duplicated audit efforts and evidence collection.
Wrong (B): Control mapping does not eliminate regulatory requirements. Each regulation still applies independently regardless of the mapping exercise.
Wrong (C): The purpose is to align existing frameworks, not to replace them with a proprietary alternative.
Wrong (D): Ignoring duplicate obligations would create compliance gaps and potential regulatory violations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3612;

-- Q3614: Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Vishing using a spoofed caller ID","Smishing via a fraudulent text message","Spear phishing using a homoglyph domain","A brute force credential stuffing attack"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-177 Rev. 1 (Trustworthy Email), spear phishing using a homoglyph domain involves registering a domain visually resembling the legitimate address by substituting similar characters (''rn'' for ''m'' in ''cornpany.com''). This targets a specific individual with a crafted, deceptive email.
Wrong (A): Vishing uses voice calls to deceive victims over the phone, not email with a look-alike domain.
Wrong (B): Smishing uses SMS text messages as the delivery channel, not email communication.
Wrong (D): Brute force attacks involve systematically guessing credentials, not crafting deceptive emails to trick a specific recipient.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3614;

-- Q3618: Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Stored cross-site scripting that persists on the server","Cross-site request forgery exploiting session tokens","Buffer overflow corrupting application memory","SQL injection targeting the backend database"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per OWASP Top 10 A03:2021 (Injection) and NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5 SI-10, stored (persistent) XSS occurs when malicious script is permanently saved on the target server and executes in every user''s browser who views the affected page. The application''s failure to sanitize input allows script injection into the database.
Wrong (B): CSRF exploits a user''s authenticated session to perform unauthorized actions but does not inject scripts that persist on the server.
Wrong (C): Buffer overflow corrupts memory to execute arbitrary code at the system level, not through browser-rendered scripts.
Wrong (D): SQL injection manipulates database queries to access or modify data, not to inject client-side scripts into web pages.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3618;

-- Q3620: Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Failure to parameterize user input before passing it to database queries","Weak password requirements on the authentication form","Lack of TLS encryption on the login page connection","Insufficient server memory allocated to the database"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per OWASP Top 10 A03:2021 (Injection) and NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5 SI-10, SQL injection occurs because the application concatenates user input directly into SQL queries without parameterization. Parameterized queries (prepared statements) separate SQL logic from user-supplied data, fundamentally preventing the database from interpreting input as executable commands.
Wrong (B): Weak passwords affect credential strength but do not cause the application to treat user input as executable SQL code.
Wrong (C): Lack of encryption exposes data in transit but does not enable SQL injection through unsanitized input fields.
Wrong (D): Insufficient memory causes performance issues, not input validation vulnerabilities that allow query manipulation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3620;

-- Q3621: Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Distributed Denial of Service attack using a botnet","Man-in-the-middle attack intercepting network traffic","Phishing campaign targeting employee credentials","Insider threat exfiltrating sensitive data"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2 (Computer Security Incident Handling Guide), a DDoS attack uses multiple compromised systems forming a botnet to flood a target with traffic, overwhelming its resources and making it unavailable. The distributed nature using thousands of devices worldwide makes DDoS significantly harder to mitigate than single-source attacks.
Wrong (B): A man-in-the-middle attack intercepts communications between two parties but does not overwhelm a target with massive traffic volume.
Wrong (C): Phishing campaigns trick users into revealing credentials or installing malware, not flooding websites with traffic.
Wrong (D): Insider threats involve authorized individuals misusing access, not external traffic floods from compromised devices.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3621;

-- Q3622: Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Volumetric attack flooding network bandwidth capacity","Application layer (Layer 7) attack targeting server resources","Protocol attack exploiting TCP/IP handshake weaknesses","Physical attack disrupting network infrastructure hardware"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, application layer (Layer 7) DDoS attacks target web applications by sending seemingly legitimate HTTP requests that consume disproportionate server resources such as CPU, memory, and database connections. These attacks are harder to detect because individual requests appear normal.
Wrong (A): Volumetric attacks overwhelm bandwidth capacity with sheer traffic volume rather than targeting specific application processing resources.
Wrong (C): Protocol attacks exploit weaknesses in network protocol implementations like TCP handshake floods, not application-level database queries.
Wrong (D): Physical attacks involve hardware tampering or destruction, not sending HTTP requests over the network.$EXPL$
